  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1132 AM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nA dreary morning and early afternoon starts the week with these\nconditions lingering through the remainder of this afternoon \ninto tonight. These dreary conditions are the result of an \nupper- level low pressure system moving across the area over a \ncool moist airmass being advected in from near the Great Lakes. \nTemperatures are currently in the upper 50s and lower 60s and \nlittle warming is anticipated through the remainder of the \nafternoon, resulting in cool highs. A cluster of showers is \ncurrently moving through northeast Nebraska. These showers \nshould gradually weaken and dissipate this afternoon, but \nadditional isolated showers may develop across the area, \nparticularly over northeast Nebraska and west- central Iowa. \nOVerall the chance of rain is low (20-40%). While the upper- \nlevel low pressure system will move east of the region tonight, \nlingering low-level moisture and low clouds may support patchy \nfog or drizzle into Tuesday morning with temperatures cooling \ninto the 50s. \n\nTuesday afternoon sees drier conditions return to the area. While \ntemperatures remain cool with plenty of lingering clouds, no rain is \nforecast. Temperatures may be a little warmer than today with highs \nin the mid 60s for many. This drier weather is short-lived as our \nnext weather system will be fast approaching. By Wednesday morning, \nwarm advection is forecast to result in a low chance (20-40%) of \nshowers and thunderstorms across central Nebraska into western \nportions of the area. This activity will wane after sunrise with \nsome dry conditions expected Wednesday afternoon. Highs will once \nagain remain in the 60s. \n\nRain chances increase on Thursday as our next weather system moves \ninto the region. Widespread showers and thunderstorms are \nanticipated with a high chance (60-80%) of rain for almost the \nentire area by weeks end. Despite the return of southeast winds, \ncontinued clouds and rain keep temperatures cooler with highs in the \nmid to upper 60s. Highs in the 70s may return by this weekend as \ncontinued south winds and filtered sunshine allow for increased \nwarming. Rain chances (30-60%) remain in the forecast during the \nweekend. A more significant weather system is currently forecast \nearly next week. This system may bring another round of rainfall, \npotentially with significant accumulations, and drop highs back into \nthe 60s.",
